# Frontend guide: middle endpoints and the bounce

Audience: the portal (frontend) developer.
Companion to [bounce-for-all-coder-apps.md](bounce-for-all-coder-apps.md), which explains the *why*; this page is the *what to call and how*.
Reference implementation: `spike-middle/portal/public/app.js` (search for `openViaBounce`).

## TL;DR

- The portal only ever talks to **middle**, with the IdP JWT as `Authorization: Bearer …`. It never sees or stores a Coder token.
- Anything that renders a Coder page in the browser (apps like VS Code Browser/Jupyter, forwarded ports, GitLab consent) is opened with **one recipe**:
  1. open a blank tab **synchronously in the click handler**,
  2. `POST /v1/bounce/start` → `{bounce_url, code}`,
  3. **form-POST** `code` to `bounce_url` in that tab.
- The `code` is single-use, lives 60 s and is bound to user, plane, workspace/app and host. Ask for a new one on **every click**; never cache, retry or reuse it.
- Stop using: `/coder-app/…`, `/open/…`, `?coder_session_token=` URLs, and the "open then strip the token" trick.
- Never display tokens. The CLI/SSH copy and VS Code Desktop flows put credentials on the clipboard or in a `vscode://` URI only.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant U as Browser (portal tab)
  participant T as New tab
  participant M as middle
  participant B as bounce (plane or app host)
  participant C as Coder
  U->>T: window.open("about:blank") (in click handler)
  U->>M: POST /v1/bounce/start (Bearer JWT)
  M-->>U: {bounce_url, code, expires_in: 60}
  U->>T: build <form method=POST action=bounce_url> code=… ; submit()
  T->>B: POST code
  B->>M: redeem code (server to server)
  B-->>T: Set-Cookie (host-only) + 302 to the app
  T->>C: app loads with its cookie
```

## 1. Authentication

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Header on every `/v1/*` call | `Authorization: Bearer <IdP JWT>` |
| Content type for bodies | `application/json` |
| Errors | non-2xx with a **plain-text** body (not JSON). Show it, don't parse it. |
| Discovery (no auth) | `GET /v1/config/public` → `{public_url, idp_issuer, email_claim, …}` |
| Health (no auth) | `GET /healthz` |

Minimal helper (same as `api()` in `app.js`):

```js
async function api(path, opts = {}) {
  const headers = { ...(opts.headers || {}) };
  headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${getIdpToken()}`;
  if (opts.body && !headers["Content-Type"]) headers["Content-Type"] = "application/json";
  const res = await fetch(`${MIDDLE_URL}${path}`, { ...opts, headers });
  const text = await res.text();
  let data;
  try { data = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null; } catch { data = text; }
  if (!res.ok) {
    const err = new Error(typeof data === "string" ? data : JSON.stringify(data));
    err.status = res.status;
    throw err;
  }
  return data;
}
```

## 2. Endpoint reference

`plane` is optional everywhere; when omitted, middle uses the default plane. For workspace calls, always pass the `plane_id` you got back in the workspace list.

| Method and path | Body / query | Response | Use it for |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /v1/me` | | `{email, coder_user}` | Header / who am I |
| `GET /v1/planes` | | `{planes: [{id, url}], default}` | Plane picker (if you show one) |
| `GET /v1/templates` | `?plane=` | `{templates: [{id, name, display_name, …}]}` | Create form |
| `GET /v1/workspaces` | `?plane=` (optional filter) | `{workspaces: [<Coder workspace> + plane_id, plane_url]}` | Workspace list |
| `POST /v1/workspaces` | `{name, template_id, plane_id}` | `201` Coder workspace + `plane_id` | Create. `name` must match `^[a-zA-Z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,31}$` |
| `DELETE /v1/workspaces/{id}` | `?plane=` | `202 {workspace_id, build}` | Delete (async build) |
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/access` | `?plane=` | see section 3 | What can the user open, and how |
| `POST /v1/bounce/start` | see section 4 | `{bounce_url, code, method, expires_in, plane_id}` | Open any Coder page |
| `GET /v1/external-auth/{provider}` | `?plane=` | `{provider_id, plane_id, authenticated, display_name, login}` | "GitLab: linked (alice)" |
| `DELETE /v1/external-auth/{provider}` | `?plane=` | `{provider_id, plane_id, authenticated: false}` | Unlink GitLab |
| `POST /v1/workspaces/{id}/vscode-desktop` | `?plane=`, body `{}` | `{uri, …}` | VS Code Desktop (section 7) |
| `GET /v1/cli-credentials` | `?plane=` | `{plane_id, coder_url, session_token, username, …}` | "Copy SSH command" (section 7) |
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/build-logs` | WebSocket, see section 8 | stream | Build progress |
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/agent-logs` | WebSocket, see section 8 | stream | Startup script progress |

The provider id for GitLab is `primary-gitlab`.

## 3. The access response: deciding what to show

`GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/access` (only meaningful when the workspace is started):

```json
{
  "workspace_id": "…", "workspace_name": "dev", "username": "alice",
  "started": true, "startup_ready": true,
  "agent_id": "…", "agent_name": "main", "agent_directory": "/home/coder",
  "display_apps": ["vscode", "web_terminal", "ssh_helper", "port_forwarding_helper"],
  "port_bounce": true,
  "choices": [
    { "id": "app:code-server", "kind": "vscode_browser", "label": "VS Code Browser",
      "slug": "code-server", "subdomain": false, "bounce": true, "available": true },
    { "id": "app:jupyter", "kind": "app", "label": "Jupyter",
      "slug": "jupyter", "subdomain": true, "bounce": true, "available": true },
    { "id": "vscode_desktop", "kind": "vscode_desktop", "label": "VS Code Desktop", "available": true },
    { "id": "ssh", "kind": "ssh", "label": "SSH", "available": true },
    { "id": "app:docs", "kind": "external_app", "label": "Docs", "slug": "docs",
      "available": false, "unavailable_reason": "External app — open from Coder dashboard" }
  ]
}
```

Rules:

- Render one button per `choices[]` entry, in order, using `label`.
- `available: false` → disabled button with `unavailable_reason` as the tooltip (e.g. "starting…", "unhealthy").
- `subdomain` is informational. The frontend does **not** branch on it; middle picks the right `bounce_url`.
- `port_bounce: true` → show a "port" input + "Open" button (section 5). If false, hide it.
- Re-fetch access after start/stop, or when the user expands a card; it is cheap.

What each `kind` does on click:

| `kind` | Condition | Action |
|---|---|---|
| `vscode_browser`, `app` | `bounce: true` | `openViaBounce({plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id, slug})` |
| `web_terminal` | | `openViaBounce({plane, purpose: "terminal", workspace_id})` (to be replaced by a `ttyd` app; see section 9) |
| `vscode_desktop` | | `POST …/vscode-desktop`, then `location.href = uri` |
| `ssh` | | `GET /v1/cli-credentials`, copy to clipboard |
| `external_app` | | disabled |

## 4. The one recipe: `openViaBounce`

Request body for `POST /v1/bounce/start`:

| Field | Required for | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `plane` | all | the workspace's `plane_id` (empty string = default plane) |
| `purpose` | all | `app`, `port`, `terminal`, `external-auth` |
| `workspace_id` | `app`, `port`, `terminal` | |
| `slug` | `app` | from `choices[].slug` |
| `port` | `port` | integer 1–65535 |
| `protocol` | `port` (optional) | `http` (default) or `https` |
| `provider` | `external-auth` | `primary-gitlab` |

Response:

```json
{ "bounce_url": "https://plane-a.example.com/_portal/bounce",
  "code": "…opaque…", "method": "POST", "expires_in": 60, "plane_id": "a" }
```

`bounce_url` is either the plane host (`/_portal/bounce`) or the app's own host (subdomain apps and ports). Treat it as opaque.

Implementation:

```js
/**
 * Opens a Coder page (app, port, GitLab consent) through the bounce.
 * Must be called directly from a click handler, before any await, so the
 * pop-up blocker allows the new tab.
 */
async function openViaBounce(body, { sameTab = false } = {}) {
  const w = sameTab ? window : window.open("about:blank", "_blank");
  if (!w) throw new Error("Pop-up blocked: allow pop-ups for this site to open workspace apps.");
  if (!sameTab) w.opener = null;
  try {
    const data = await api("/v1/bounce/start", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(body) });
    const doc = w.document;
    const form = doc.createElement("form");
    form.method = "POST";
    form.action = data.bounce_url;
    const input = doc.createElement("input");
    input.type = "hidden";
    input.name = "code";
    input.value = data.code;
    form.appendChild(input);
    doc.body.appendChild(form);
    form.submit();
  } catch (e) {
    if (!sameTab) w.close();
    throw e;
  }
}
```

Do:

- Call `window.open` **before** the `await` (browsers only allow pop-ups during the user gesture).
- Use a real form POST so the code never appears in a URL, history, or `Referer`.
- Get a fresh code for every click, including retries.

Don't:

- `fetch()` the `bounce_url` (it needs a top-level navigation to set the cookie; `fetch` fails on CORS and cookies).
- Put the code in a query string (`?code=` works as a fallback for tests only).
- Open apps in an `<iframe>` in the portal (third-party cookies and Coder's frame headers will break it).
- Log `code` or keep it in state.

If the bounce itself fails (code expired, used twice, plane mismatch), the new tab shows a short plain-text error from the bounce. The fix for the user is always "click again".

## 5. Forwarded ports

Shown only when `port_bounce: true`.

```js
openPortBtn.onclick = async () => {
  const port = Number(portInput.value);
  if (!Number.isInteger(port) || port < 1 || port > 65535) return alert("Enter a port between 1 and 65535");
  await openViaBounce({ plane: ws.plane_id, purpose: "port", workspace_id: ws.id, port /*, protocol: "https" */ });
};
```

Note the validation must happen **before** `openViaBounce` (so you don't open a blank tab for a bad input), but `openViaBounce` itself must still be the first `await` in the handler.

## 6. Connect / disconnect GitLab (external auth)

GitLab consent is a Coder page that needs a cookie, so it goes through the bounce too, but in the **same tab** (the flow ends back on the portal).

```js
const PROVIDER = "primary-gitlab";

async function refreshGitLab() {
  const st = await api(`/v1/external-auth/${PROVIDER}`);
  gitlabBtn.textContent = st.authenticated ? `GitLab: linked${st.login ? ` (${st.login})` : ""}` : "Connect GitLab";
  gitlabBtn.dataset.linked = st.authenticated ? "1" : "";
}

gitlabBtn.onclick = async () => {
  if (gitlabBtn.dataset.linked) {
    if (confirm("Disconnect GitLab?")) {
      await api(`/v1/external-auth/${PROVIDER}`, { method: "DELETE" });
      await refreshGitLab();
    }
    return;
  }
  await openViaBounce({ plane: "", purpose: "external-auth", provider: PROVIDER }, { sameTab: true });
};

// Coder → {plane}/_portal/done → back to the portal with ?linked=1
if (new URLSearchParams(location.search).has("linked")) {
  history.replaceState(null, "", location.pathname);
}
refreshGitLab();
```

Flow: portal → bounce (plane host) → Coder consent → GitLab → Coder → `/_portal/done` (clears the temporary cookie, revokes the temporary key) → portal `?linked=1`. After landing, just re-read the status; don't trust `?linked=1` as proof.

Because it navigates away in the same tab, make sure the IdP session/JWT survives a full page reload (store it the way you already do for refreshes).

## 7. CLI, SSH and VS Code Desktop

These do not use the bounce; they need a token on the user's machine.

- **Copy SSH command** (`kind: "ssh"`): `GET /v1/cli-credentials?plane=…`, build the text below, put it on the clipboard, and show "Copied". Never render the token in the page.

  ```js
  const c = await api(`/v1/cli-credentials?plane=${encodeURIComponent(ws.plane_id)}`);
  await navigator.clipboard.writeText(
    `export CODER_URL='${c.coder_url}'\nexport CODER_SESSION_TOKEN='${c.session_token}'\ncoder ssh ${ws.name}`
  );
  ```

- **VS Code Desktop** (`kind: "vscode_desktop"`): `POST /v1/workspaces/{id}/vscode-desktop?plane=…` with `{}`, then `window.location.href = data.uri` (a `vscode://coder.coder-remote/open?…` link). Ignore the other fields; don't display them.

Backend note (no frontend change): these tokens are being moved to **scoped** tokens (`coder:workspaces.access` + `user:read`, validated with `coder ssh` and VS Code Desktop). The response shapes stay the same.

## 8. Build and startup logs

Browsers can't set `Authorization` on a WebSocket, so logs currently use a short-lived middle ticket:

1. `POST /v1/app-session` with `{}` → `{middle_token}`.
2. Open `wss://<middle>/v1/workspaces/{id}/build-logs?mt=<middle_token>&after=-1&build=<latest_build.id>` or `…/agent-logs?mt=<middle_token>&after=0`.
3. Each message is one JSON log line (Coder's format). Close the socket when the build completes or the card collapses.

This is the one place still using `mt`. It will change (the design doc plans to remove `middle_session`/`?mt=`); keep the code isolated in one function so it's easy to swap.

## 9. Deprecated: stop using these

| Old | Replace with |
|---|---|
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/coder-app/{slug}` (+ `?format=json`) | `openViaBounce({purpose: "app", slug})` |
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/open/…` | `openViaBounce` |
| URLs containing `coder_session_token=`, and the "open then `location.replace` to strip the token" helper | `openViaBounce` (no token in any URL) |
| `POST /v1/app-session` for opening apps, `middle_session` cookie | Bearer JWT + bounce (still used only for logs, section 8) |
| `kind: "terminal"` (ttyd via `coder-app`) | an app choice with slug `ttyd` via `purpose: "app"` |
| `purpose: "terminal"` (Coder web terminal) | will be retired in favour of the `ttyd` app; keep it working until the templates ship `ttyd` |
| Showing the Coder URL or token anywhere | clipboard / `vscode://` only |

## 10. Errors from `POST /v1/bounce/start` (and friends)

The body is plain text; show it as-is in a toast, then map the status to the user message:

| Status | Meaning | Suggested UI |
|---|---|---|
| `400` | bad input (unknown purpose, bad slug, port out of range, bad provider) | "Invalid request" + text (a frontend bug) |
| `401` | JWT missing/expired | re-login |
| `403` | not your workspace | "You don't have access to this workspace" |
| `404` | workspace or app not found | refresh the list |
| `409` | workspace not running, no agent yet, or app unhealthy (`workspace is stopped`, `app is unhealthy`) | "Workspace is starting, try again in a moment", or offer Start |
| `501` | bounce not configured on this plane, or subdomain apps not enabled there | "Not available on this plane" (disable the button) |
| `502` | middle couldn't reach Coder | "Try again" |

A failure on `/v1/bounce/start` closes the blank tab automatically (see the `catch` in `openViaBounce`).

## 11. Checklist for the portal

- [ ] All calls go to middle with `Authorization: Bearer <JWT>`; no Coder URLs are built client-side.
- [ ] Buttons are driven by `GET …/access` (`choices`, `available`, `unavailable_reason`, `port_bounce`).
- [ ] Every "open" goes through `openViaBounce`, with `window.open` before the first `await`.
- [ ] A new code is requested per click; the code is never logged, stored, or put in a URL.
- [ ] Ports input validated before opening; `protocol` only when the service is HTTPS.
- [ ] GitLab connect in the same tab; status re-read on return; `?linked=1` removed from the URL.
- [ ] SSH/CLI and VS Code Desktop never render tokens.
- [ ] The log WebSocket code is isolated (the `mt` ticket will be replaced).
- [ ] No calls to the deprecated endpoints in section 9.
